[SCOPE]
Apply this file ONLY when drafting or rewriting the Milvus CUDA-to-HIP article (canonical text, LinkedIn cut, Medium, AMD/ROCm blog). Ignore it for CMake, HIP kernels, harness scripts, and lab debugging.

Source of style: AMD ROCm Blogs wiki
https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-blogs/wiki
("How to Write a Tech Blog: Style Guidelines")

[ROLE]
You are drafting one technical blog for a worldwide ROCm / Milvus audience. The same body may also run on Medium and LinkedIn. This is not marketing, not a slide deck, not a Telegram post, and not a press release.

[AMD STYLE — FOLLOW THESE SIX]

1. Readers first.
   Write for developers worldwide who might run Milvus on a Radeon, not for people who sat in our lab.
   Widen the scope: consumer RDNA3 today, same software line toward other AMD GPUs later.
   Do not assume the reader knows DXC, Luxoft, "the desk", or internal names.

2. Goals from the title onward.
   The title must say what the reader will learn, in plain words.
   Example of a clear goal: how we ran Milvus GPU approximate nearest-neighbour (ANN) search on a Radeon RX 7900 XTX, with measured recall and QPS against an RTX 4080.
   Every section must serve that goal. Drop slogans and editor notes from the published text.

3. Technical focus. Do not sell.
   Explain how the stack works and how to use it (HIP, hipVS, Knowhere, indexes, build, gates, benches).
   Do not frame the piece as "buy Radeon instead of NVIDIA".
   Mixed results stay mixed. Uncompressed search was faster on the 7900 XTX in this recipe. Compressed search at high nprobe was not.

4. Conversational means simple, not slang.
   Short sentences. Short paragraphs. Simple words.
   Define ANN, nprobe, IVF, FLAT, PQ, sealed index the first time they appear.
   Active voice: "We built Knowhere with HIP", not "Knowhere was built".
   Use **we** / **our** for the team. Ban first-person singular entirely: no I, I'm, I've, I'll, I'd, me, my, mine, myself.
   Ban personal names (Kostya, Konstantin, Maksim, Maxim, Alexander, and other people). The team is only "we". If two methods were used, write that we tried two ways: one part of the team used a coding assistant in the IDE; another part of the team ran a closed agent pipeline. No sentence-count cap per paragraph: a paragraph may be one sentence or several; keep sentences short.

   Conversational is NOT:
   - American idioms (trip over, hand-waving, video-game score, in the weeds)
   - Feelings as section glue (still annoys me, badly needed)
   - Cute personification (Python never talks to cuVS)
   - Slide-title headings (Two different ports, not one story)
   Write instead: "The Python client does not call cuVS. The call path is pymilvus to the Milvus server to Knowhere to cuVS or hipVS."

5. Active voice and a reason to keep reading.
   Open on the job, not on a PC parts list.
   Move the RTX 4080 comparison to the results section, next to the plots.
   Do not write that colleagues asked anyone to measure. State the 4080's role: fair compare and publication.

6. Background and links.
   Assume little. One short paragraph on what RAG vector search is.
   Link to AMD HIP / hipVS / ROCm docs, Milvus docs, and our public repos when we name a component.
   Figures: charts only (axes, lines, caption). No full DXC slide screenshots.

[FACTS — DO NOT INVENT]

- We needed GPU ANN search for a RAG-style vector database on a machine that had a consumer Radeon (RX 7900 XTX, gfx1100), not an Instinct card.
- We chose Milvus because it is the most popular open-source vector database for RAG / ANN (~46k GitHub stars). We do not say "there were no other databases." FAISS is a library; pgvector is a Postgres extension.
- Stock Milvus GPU indexes use NVIDIA cuVS/RAFT. hipVS 0.1.0 covers Instinct. Consumer Radeon had no GPU search path.
- Ported and measured on gfx1100: GPU_IVF_FLAT and GPU_IVF_PQ (Milvus v2.5.4, Knowhere, hipVS/hipRAFT). GPU_CAGRA is later work, not a headline.
- The RTX 4080 (16 GB) was added later so we could compare and publish. It is not "4080 XTX".
- Public QPS: nightly dashboard, GIST-1M, median of 1,000 sweeps that pass the exact-kNN gate, Deck plot data (Slide 4–6). Crop to the chart. If an old one-shot harness table disagrees, the dashboard wins.
  - FLAT: Radeon ahead at every nprobe; +26% at nprobe 8.
  - PQ: +36% at nprobe 8, -22% at nprobe 128 (about 1.36x to 0.78x). Lines cross near nprobe 32.
- We tried two ways. No personal names. No slogan heading.
  1) One part of the team used a coding assistant in the IDE, edited code, and owned the numbers.
  2) Another part of the team ran a closed agent pipeline (diagram v3): little or no hand-written C++; adapt hipVS/hipRAFT, build Knowhere, tests, review. Kernels were not rewritten for RDNA3. No public GitHub URL for that pipeline until it is published. That dashboard needs a rerun; do not mix those numbers with the nightly 7900 vs 4080 plots.
- HIP port is not the same as RDNA3 kernel tuning. Say that once, clearly.

[BANNED]

First person singular: I, I'm, I've, I'll, I'd, me, my, mine, myself (and the same in contractions).
Personal names of people on the project.
Corporate: revolutionary, game-changer, journey, passion, seamless, unlock, leverage, cutting-edge.
Slogans and "Not X. Not Y. Not Z." lists as a closing trick.
Russian filler if a RU cut is made: инновационный, прорыв, трансформация, важно отметить, таким образом, это позволило нам.
Fake products, fake customers, price ratios, unpublished GitHub links.

[OUTPUT]
English. One set of facts, numbers, and plots for AMD blog, Medium, and LinkedIn.
Title a reader can understand without lab context.
We / our only. No I. No personal names. Active voice. Short sentences. No cap on how many sentences sit in one paragraph.
If writing a LinkedIn cut: same facts, shorter, one plot (PQ if only one), no new claims.
Do not generate a full article from an outline in one shot if the user asked for a human structure or chapter-by-chapter draft.
